1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,360
Hey friends and welcome to the Riker Medical Podcast.

2
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:09,640
Riker Medical is a platform for education focused on emergency, transport, and critical

3
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:15,280
care medicine.

4
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:20,360
We believe that the sharing of knowledge should be free, accessible to all, and that innovative

5
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:23,860
use of technology can facilitate that effort.

6
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:29,060
This podcast is just one piece of that whole project.

7
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:36,960
Also, while we may mention medical treatments and interventions along the way, remember

8
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:40,700
that we are just strangers on the internet and don't do things you aren't allowed to

9
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:45,860
do.

10
00:00:45,860 --> 00:01:14,860
For more information about the project, visit us at RikerMedical.com.

11
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:18,740
Hey friends and welcome back to the Riker Medical Podcast.

12
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:23,340
Today we are going to jump into a clinical subject and discuss patient assessment in

13
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:25,500
critical care transport.

14
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:29,420
The background on this is that we've gotten the question a handful of times from our nursing

15
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:33,300
friends, how do you know what to do when you get a sick patient?

16
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:38,260
EMS folks across the board are pretty good at managing the initial steps in assessing

17
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:42,980
and treating critically ill or injured patients due to the specific way we teach and practice

18
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:44,620
patient assessment.

19
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:47,940
This is somewhat different than the way nurses are taught assessment.

20
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:52,820
EMS offers an algorithmic approach to patient assessment that is fairly consistent across

21
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:55,420
different programs and institutions.

22
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:59,480
It guides clinicians in the initial approach to both trauma and medical patients presenting

23
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:02,340
on scene with acute injuries or illness.

24
00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:06,980
The nursing field, on the other hand, is a bit less consistent in its approach to patient

25
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:12,260
assessment and tends to teach it via a collection of different components, one of which is the

26
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:14,380
systems-based approach.

27
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:19,220
That said, this method of organizing a patient assessment and some of the specific components

28
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:22,580
do have value in the CCT world.

29
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:27,580
The general idea with this episode is to explain how patient assessment in EMS works for those

30
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:31,340
who are unfamiliar with it and then to do the same for one of the ways assessment is

31
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:33,120
taught in nursing.

32
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:37,420
Both approaches have value and the end result is a sort of hybrid that works well in the

33
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:39,260
critical care transport setting.

34
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:44,340
We'll also mention some strategies for practice and betterment so that listeners can dial

35
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:45,980
in their assessment techniques.

36
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:52,740
The foundation of patient assessment in EMS comes from a template used by the National

37
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:57,860
Registry of EMTs to evaluate EMS providers during a cycle motor assessment for initial

38
00:02:57,860 --> 00:02:59,340
certification.

39
00:02:59,340 --> 00:03:03,740
The way it works is that the inner EMT has a skill sheet that proctors use to verify

40
00:03:03,740 --> 00:03:08,820
competency as EMS students move through the assessment and management of patients in simulated

41
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:10,300
scenarios.

42
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:14,980
They basically mark off line items in a checklist and a score is determined by how many of those

43
00:03:14,980 --> 00:03:17,020
things are performed correctly.

44
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:22,260
Now, this approach as both a learning tool and a method for the evaluation of entry-level

45
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:27,020
providers has come under scrutiny, but the point still stands that the vast majority

46
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:31,620
of EMS programs utilize this framework to teach patient assessment.

47
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:35,820
And the result of this is that most EMS providers use, or were at least taught during initial

48
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:40,340
training, a similar sequence of steps for patient assessment.

49
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:44,940
It's probably easier to appreciate how this works in a video format, so we'll add some

50
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:47,700
links in the show notes for those who are interested.

51
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:50,460
But here's our audio version of how it all works.

52
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:55,660
We'll start with an unresponsive trauma assessment and then can discuss the differences for medical

53
00:03:55,660 --> 00:03:59,860
patients and responsive trauma patients in just a second.

54
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:04,820
The first step is the Scene Size Up or Scene Survey, a series of steps that happens even

55
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:07,740
before patient contact is made.

56
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:12,060
Scene Safety and BSI is the initial catchphrase most often heard.

57
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:16,660
It includes ensuring that the scene is safe for EMS providers to operate in and then utilizing

58
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:21,940
body substance isolation, BSI, techniques to protect oneself.

59
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:27,340
This essentially means putting on gloves, but also considering other PPE as appropriate.

60
00:04:27,340 --> 00:04:33,500
Next, we consider Mechanism of Injury or Nature of Illness, how the patient in question transitioned

61
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:39,980
from normal human to patient, i.e. what happened to them to prompt our arrival on scene.

62
00:04:39,980 --> 00:04:42,060
After that is Number of Patients.

63
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:47,140
We want to avoid missing patients by focusing only on the first thing we see on scene.

64
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:50,020
Then we consider the need for additional resources.

65
00:04:50,020 --> 00:04:55,700
This could be more ambulances, air medical, fire and rescue, law enforcement, technical

66
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:58,500
rescue, whatever might be necessary.

67
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:02,540
All of these things are subject to change as new information becomes available, but

68
00:05:02,540 --> 00:05:06,460
the thought here is to think about them all early on before we get distracted by patient

69
00:05:06,460 --> 00:05:07,740
care.

70
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:11,820
And then lastly, we want to consider the need for cervical spine precautions, whether that

71
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:16,580
be immobilization for those stuck in the past or spinal motion restriction for the cool

72
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:18,900
kids.

73
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:23,600
After the Scene Size Up or Scene Survey is the Primary Assessment, Primary Survey or

74
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:25,100
Initial Assessment.

75
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:28,180
All these names refer to the same thing.

76
00:05:28,180 --> 00:05:31,940
First step is to form a general impression as we enter the scene and lay eyes on the

77
00:05:31,940 --> 00:05:32,940
patient.

78
00:05:32,940 --> 00:05:38,020
This is often described as the initial sick or not sick determination and is based solely

79
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:40,980
on how the patient looks at first sight.

80
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:45,460
This is similar to the idea of the Across the Room Assessment for Pediatric, referred

81
00:05:45,460 --> 00:05:48,060
to as the Pediatric Assessment Triangle in PALS.

82
00:05:48,060 --> 00:05:51,420
We'll come back to that idea later on.

83
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:56,980
After that, we introduce ourselves and determine a level of responsiveness or consciousness.

84
00:05:56,980 --> 00:06:00,820
Next it's to figure out what is going on with the patient by either asking them or

85
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:04,260
looking for immediately life-threatening injuries.

86
00:06:04,260 --> 00:06:10,160
And then we move into the classic ABCs, airway, breathing and circulation.

87
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:16,060
Airway entails opening the airway if needed and placing an NPA or OPA as indicated.

88
00:06:16,060 --> 00:06:21,180
Breathing includes assessing breathing, ensuring adequate ventilation, providing oxygen and

89
00:06:21,180 --> 00:06:26,980
then fixing things that might obstruct breathing, such as decompressing attention pneumothorax.

90
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:32,580
And then circulation involves three assessments, checking a pulse, rate, rhythm and quality,

91
00:06:32,580 --> 00:06:37,780
checking the skin, color, temperature and condition, and performing a blood sweep.

92
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:41,620
The blood sweep is where we expose the patient and check for life-threatening bleeds that

93
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:44,100
we missed earlier on in the assessment.

94
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:47,600
And if noted, we treat those immediately.

95
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:50,520
Circulation also includes treating for shock.

96
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:54,920
The specifics of that vary depending on the level of the provider, but at a minimum we

97
00:06:54,920 --> 00:06:57,280
teach to keep the patient warm.

98
00:06:57,280 --> 00:07:01,440
And then the last step in the primary is to determine a transport priority.

99
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:05,600
This is essentially deciding if the patient needs to get moving as soon as possible or

100
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:09,840
if there's time to complete an assessment and perform interventions on scene prior to

101
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:11,840
transport.

102
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:16,720
Next on the NREMT assessment for trauma patient is to get vital signs and obtain what we call

103
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:18,860
a sample history.

104
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:21,660
Final signs should be familiar to everyone listening.

105
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:25,880
Sample is a mnemonic to walk us through specific things we want to know about a patient's.

106
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:32,960
It stands for signs and symptoms, allergies, medications, past medical history, last oral

107
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:36,460
intake and events leading up to the incident.

108
00:07:36,460 --> 00:07:40,440
It's a simple way for us to gather some basic need to know information about a patient's

109
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:43,340
medical history and what is currently going on.

110
00:07:43,340 --> 00:07:48,060
This may or may not be possible to obtain, but per the assessment checklist, we at least

111
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:53,140
make an attempt to do so or acknowledge that it isn't possible.

112
00:07:53,140 --> 00:07:58,240
The final area of focus in the trauma assessment is the head to toe or secondary assessment.

113
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:01,340
This might be done on scene or it might be done in transport.

114
00:08:01,340 --> 00:08:05,120
There are also cases where it never gets done because we get caught up with managing life

115
00:08:05,120 --> 00:08:07,740
threats noted in the primary survey.

116
00:08:07,740 --> 00:08:11,660
But in testing and in practice, we do work through all the steps, so we'll give a quick

117
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:13,420
overview here.

118
00:08:13,420 --> 00:08:17,500
We won't get into all the details and, again, it may make more sense to check out one of

119
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:21,940
the linked videos, but this step basically includes getting our hands on the patient,

120
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:25,880
starting at the head, working down the body to the extremities, and then the back as we

121
00:08:25,880 --> 00:08:27,300
roll the patient.

122
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:32,140
The idea is to systematically assess each part of the patient's body looking for specific

123
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:34,500
things in each region.

124
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:40,580
So for example, when we assess the head, the specific steps include inspecting the head,

125
00:08:40,580 --> 00:08:44,340
palpating the skull, assessing the pupils for reactivity.

126
00:08:44,340 --> 00:08:48,580
Some folks may also include things like peeking in the eyes and nose and looking into the

127
00:08:48,580 --> 00:08:52,460
mouth again, which we already did when we assessed airway.

128
00:08:52,460 --> 00:08:56,900
After that, we'd move on to the neck, checking to make sure the trachea is midline, assessing

129
00:08:56,900 --> 00:09:03,220
for JVD or jugular vein distension, palpating the cervical spine at the back of the neck.

130
00:09:03,220 --> 00:09:09,120
Then we'll move on to the chest, abdomen, pelvis, etc. on down the body.

131
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:13,740
During the secondary assessment, if we come across life-threatening injuries, we definitely

132
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:16,380
stop and treat those as they are found.

133
00:09:16,380 --> 00:09:20,740
For everything else, however, we defer treatment until the end.

134
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:24,640
Things like dressings for superficial bleeding or splints can wait until the assessment is

135
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:28,000
done and may even get deferred altogether.

136
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:32,780
The idea here is to identify abnormal findings and injuries and to establish a baseline to

137
00:09:32,780 --> 00:09:36,700
which we can compare subsequent findings on assessment.

138
00:09:36,700 --> 00:09:41,740
So we work our way down the body, head to foot, go back and assess the arms, then roll

139
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:44,480
the patient over and do the back.

140
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:50,280
After that, we give treatments and perform interventions as we have time and as is indicated.

141
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:54,720
And that's how we do what we do, according to the NREMT and the skill sheets most of

142
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:56,460
us learned on.

143
00:09:56,460 --> 00:09:58,680
A few notes on all of this.

144
00:09:58,680 --> 00:10:02,960
First is that there is one step about reassessing the patient either after interventions or

145
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:04,000
in route.

146
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:05,600
Don't forget to do that.

147
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:11,240
Second, the value in a system like this is that it forces the user to look for specific,

148
00:10:11,240 --> 00:10:14,220
different things at or in specific parts of the body.

149
00:10:14,220 --> 00:10:17,600
But that whole approach hinges on sticking to the system.

150
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:20,540
If we deviate from the system, we miss things.

151
00:10:20,540 --> 00:10:24,460
So either stick to the system or come up with a better system, which we'll discuss in just

152
00:10:24,460 --> 00:10:25,460
a few.

153
00:10:25,460 --> 00:10:28,520
Third, go back to the top if you get lost.

154
00:10:28,520 --> 00:10:33,020
You can always start back at the top with how does the patient look and the ABCs and

155
00:10:33,020 --> 00:10:34,620
then carry on from there.

156
00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:38,420
That's a totally acceptable and an encouraged strategy.

157
00:10:39,140 --> 00:10:44,880
Now, this design for patient assessment does seem to work best with the unresponsive trauma

158
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:45,880
patient.

159
00:10:45,880 --> 00:10:47,740
So that's why we started there.

160
00:10:47,740 --> 00:10:51,680
It gets a little bit weird when you have a patient with an isolated injury who is both

161
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:54,560
reliable and participatory.

162
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:59,280
It's also a bit funky with medical cases as the history gathering and focus assessment

163
00:10:59,280 --> 00:11:01,720
make the system less rigid.

164
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:06,140
The idea there is that it may not make sense and it may be a bit awkward to work through

165
00:11:06,140 --> 00:11:09,460
a head to toe assessment on a patient who doesn't need it.

166
00:11:09,460 --> 00:11:14,640
That decision comes with experience and some outfits or programs teach this concept differently.

167
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:18,800
But if you're not sure, just stick with the system and do a complete assessment as outlined

168
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:20,160
in the skill sheet.

169
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:23,000
And that's often the way we teach it at basic levels.

170
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,280
Perform the head to toe on everyone in order to dial in the technique and ensure that nothing

171
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:29,120
gets missed.

172
00:11:29,120 --> 00:11:33,480
For the sake of illustrating this idea, let's consider a patient who presents with an isolated

173
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:35,080
leg injury.

174
00:11:35,080 --> 00:11:38,680
The initial steps of the whole process are the exact same.

175
00:11:38,680 --> 00:11:40,260
Start with the scene size up.

176
00:11:40,260 --> 00:11:46,920
Scene safety, BSI, mechanism of injury, number of patients, additional resources, cervical

177
00:11:46,920 --> 00:11:51,920
spine precautions, then the primary survey or initial assessment.

178
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:59,040
General impression, level of consciousness, chief complaint, ABC's, transport decision.

179
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:03,360
Baseline vitals and history are the same as for the unresponsive patient except that we'll

180
00:12:03,360 --> 00:12:05,680
actually be able to collect on it.

181
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:09,520
The next bit is where things deviate from the plan.

182
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:12,960
Performing a complete secondary or head to toe assessment on someone who is alert and

183
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:17,740
oriented when there's no suspicion of other injuries may not be warranted.

184
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:21,480
In that case, we can focus in on the region of the body affected.

185
00:12:21,480 --> 00:12:24,840
If it's the leg that's injured, we need to get eyes and hands on there.

186
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:31,120
Expose, look, touch, and assess for specific things like pulses, motor function, and sensation

187
00:12:31,120 --> 00:12:33,700
just as you would in a full head to toe.

188
00:12:33,700 --> 00:12:37,480
If we decide that things aren't as expected and there is more going on than initially

189
00:12:37,480 --> 00:12:43,040
thought, we can start back at the top or perform that head to toe at any time.

190
00:12:43,040 --> 00:12:46,640
The medical patient is also less straightforward than the unresponsive trauma patient we started

191
00:12:46,640 --> 00:12:49,520
with but in a different sort of way.

192
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:54,320
With the truly sick or unresponsive medical patient, we start with the scene size up and

193
00:12:54,320 --> 00:12:59,000
the primary and then proceed to a head to toe or secondary assessment that goes systematically

194
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,440
from superior to inferior.

195
00:13:01,440 --> 00:13:05,480
The only difference here is that it might matter a bit more whether or not we start

196
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:11,160
with the head to toe or whether we start by gathering a history to figure out the backstory.

197
00:13:11,160 --> 00:13:15,700
It is important to note that this decision comes after the primary survey or initial

198
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:20,140
assessment where we hopefully identify immediate life threats.

199
00:13:20,140 --> 00:13:22,400
That bit always comes first.

200
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:26,680
But at the point after we've done that primary survey, the decision is whether to move on

201
00:13:26,680 --> 00:13:32,400
to a history gathering series of questions or perform a detailed assessment.

202
00:13:32,400 --> 00:13:36,560
One rule of thumb here is that we do a head to toe first on the trauma patient and then

203
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:41,000
proceed to the history and that we gather a history first on the medical patient and

204
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:44,000
then proceed to do a head to toe afterwards.

205
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:48,240
You can think of this as an assessment triangle as it's taught quite often in the outdoor

206
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:49,880
medicine community.

207
00:13:49,880 --> 00:13:52,080
At the top is the primary.

208
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:56,560
On one side is the head to toe or physical and on the other is the history.

209
00:13:56,560 --> 00:14:01,560
The trauma pathway takes us around the triangle in one direction from primary to physical

210
00:14:01,560 --> 00:14:06,820
to history and the medical pathway goes in the opposite direction from primary to history

211
00:14:06,820 --> 00:14:08,120
to physical.

212
00:14:08,120 --> 00:14:11,960
If that's tough to follow, we'll add a graphic in the show notes that might make it a bit

213
00:14:11,960 --> 00:14:13,920
clearer.

214
00:14:13,920 --> 00:14:17,320
And then if we have a more stable medical patient that doesn't warrant a complete head

215
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:21,040
to toe or secondary, we can perform a focused assessment.

216
00:14:21,040 --> 00:14:25,360
A good practice is to always get hands on the patient even if it seems like we can figure

217
00:14:25,360 --> 00:14:28,360
out the story from a well-gathered history.

218
00:14:28,360 --> 00:14:33,200
And then the very last thing to mention about assessment in EMS is how we do the history

219
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:34,840
gathering bit.

220
00:14:34,840 --> 00:14:38,760
This includes two components, sample and OPQRST.

221
00:14:38,760 --> 00:14:44,600
Sample, just to review since we mentioned this before, stands for signs and symptoms,

222
00:14:44,600 --> 00:14:50,240
allergies, medications, past medical history, last oral intake, and events leading up to

223
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:51,600
the incident.

224
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:56,040
It's something we teach that needs to be gathered for all patients if possible.

225
00:14:56,040 --> 00:15:04,600
OPQRST stands for onset, provocation or palliation, quality, radiation, severity, and time.

226
00:15:04,600 --> 00:15:09,560
We use that to delve into the details when someone is complaining of pain.

227
00:15:09,560 --> 00:15:13,480
So for example, the series of questions might go like this.

228
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:15,680
Onset, how did the pain start?

229
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:17,440
Was there a precipitating event?

230
00:15:17,440 --> 00:15:20,360
Was it gradual or sudden?

231
00:15:20,360 --> 00:15:23,360
Radiation and palliation, does anything make it better?

232
00:15:23,360 --> 00:15:25,680
Does anything make it worse?

233
00:15:25,680 --> 00:15:29,160
Quality, can you describe the pain for me?

234
00:15:29,160 --> 00:15:32,480
Radiation, do you feel it anywhere else?

235
00:15:32,480 --> 00:15:37,720
Severity, on a scale from 0 to 10, 10 being getting your arms ripped off by Tyrannosaurus

236
00:15:37,720 --> 00:15:43,360
Rex and 0 being chilling on the couch watching TV, how bad is your pain?

237
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:46,080
And time, when did the pain begin?

238
00:15:46,320 --> 00:15:52,240
OPQRST can also be used to get details of a non-pain complaint, and there are also other

239
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:56,680
memory tools for specific complaints in patient presentations, but we won't get into all those

240
00:15:56,680 --> 00:15:57,800
details here.

241
00:15:59,840 --> 00:16:05,840
So to summarize how we do assessment in EMS, the system starts the same for all patients.

242
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:12,360
Scene size up includes scene safety, BSI, mechanism of injury or nature of illness,

243
00:16:12,360 --> 00:16:20,160
number of patients, need for additional resources, and consideration of cervical spine precautions.

244
00:16:20,160 --> 00:16:25,320
Primary survey or initial assessment includes general impression, level of consciousness

245
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:32,500
or level of responsiveness, chief complaint or life threats, ABCs, and transport priority.

246
00:16:32,500 --> 00:16:37,480
We then move to either a physical exam in the case of trauma or a history with medical

247
00:16:37,480 --> 00:16:38,480
patients.

248
00:16:38,480 --> 00:16:42,040
For whichever of those, we close the loop and do the history gathering for the trauma

249
00:16:42,040 --> 00:16:47,260
patient or the physical exam for the medical patient, and it's up to our discretion whether

250
00:16:47,260 --> 00:16:54,160
or not a complete head-to-toe is warranted or if a focused exam is sufficient.

251
00:16:54,160 --> 00:16:58,840
Patient assessment in nursing, on the other hand, is taught in a few different ways.

252
00:16:58,840 --> 00:17:02,820
There are courses within the nursing field that do teach algorithm-based assessments

253
00:17:02,820 --> 00:17:07,920
similar to how we do in EMS, but those tend to be specialized in the field of emergency

254
00:17:07,920 --> 00:17:08,920
medicine.

255
00:17:08,920 --> 00:17:14,440
The best example of this is TNCC with their extended ABCs algorithm to guide assessment

256
00:17:14,440 --> 00:17:16,960
and initial steps in resuscitation.

257
00:17:16,960 --> 00:17:20,560
We'll add a link to an overview of that for those of you who are curious to know what

258
00:17:20,560 --> 00:17:21,960
that's all about.

259
00:17:21,960 --> 00:17:27,400
But aside from specific courses, nursing tends to teach assessment via model much more focused

260
00:17:27,400 --> 00:17:31,000
on either primary care or acute in-hospital care.

261
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:34,460
There are many different ways that assessment is taught in nursing, but one that is quite

262
00:17:34,460 --> 00:17:40,140
common and of value to us in the CCT world is a systems-based approach.

263
00:17:40,140 --> 00:17:45,660
A systems-based approach to assessment focuses on each body system one at a time in an effort

264
00:17:45,660 --> 00:17:48,340
to understand what is going on with a patient.

265
00:17:48,340 --> 00:17:53,080
This framework for assessment isn't commonly discussed in EMS, but is likely familiar to

266
00:17:53,080 --> 00:17:56,100
anyone who has worked in acute care in the hospital.

267
00:17:56,100 --> 00:18:00,660
It's often how we structure handover reports between shifts and units, especially in the

268
00:18:00,660 --> 00:18:01,660
ICU.

269
00:18:02,180 --> 00:18:07,100
A partial example of a handoff in an ICU might sound something like this.

270
00:18:07,100 --> 00:18:10,200
We have Mr. Smith in 209, neuro.

271
00:18:10,200 --> 00:18:14,340
He sedated on propofol with fentanyl for pain, occasionally gets agitated with movements

272
00:18:14,340 --> 00:18:17,340
or turning, wrist restraints in place.

273
00:18:17,340 --> 00:18:21,340
He does withdraw from pain and opens eyes to stimuli but does not track.

274
00:18:21,340 --> 00:18:23,220
Pupils equal and reactive.

275
00:18:23,220 --> 00:18:27,980
Respiratory, 7.5 ET tube in place, 23 centimeters at the teeth.

276
00:18:27,980 --> 00:18:32,820
AC-VC at 18 and 450, 40 percent, 5 a peep.

277
00:18:32,820 --> 00:18:36,140
Synchronous at rest, sometimes sugars with movement.

278
00:18:36,140 --> 00:18:40,420
Lung sound clear, chest x-ray looks good, hope to extubate soon.

279
00:18:40,420 --> 00:18:44,660
GI, decreased bowel sounds but did have his first bowel movement last night.

280
00:18:44,660 --> 00:18:47,220
Was small and more continuing with his regimen.

281
00:18:47,220 --> 00:18:53,140
GU, fully in place, clear yellow urine, responds well to his Lasix, and on and on.

282
00:18:53,140 --> 00:18:57,700
No need for us to continue with all this as you probably get the idea.

283
00:18:57,700 --> 00:19:02,220
We can then take this same idea and apply it to assessment at the bedside.

284
00:19:02,220 --> 00:19:06,900
This is particularly useful during inter-facility transports where we don't have the urgent

285
00:19:06,900 --> 00:19:13,300
need of immediately assessing a new situation for which the EMS system we discussed is designed.

286
00:19:13,300 --> 00:19:17,220
It can also help on scene runs when somebody else has been looking after the patient for

287
00:19:17,220 --> 00:19:20,740
a while and already has things more or less sorted.

288
00:19:20,740 --> 00:19:25,740
We can even work it into our algorithm somehow after a history and physical or as a framework

289
00:19:25,740 --> 00:19:27,980
for reassessments in route.

290
00:19:27,980 --> 00:19:31,860
The basic idea is that it's another way to organize patient assessment in a bit more

291
00:19:31,860 --> 00:19:34,820
detail when time allows.

292
00:19:34,820 --> 00:19:39,700
But in order to do a systems-based assessment on a patient, we need to be able to recall

293
00:19:39,700 --> 00:19:41,980
all the body systems.

294
00:19:41,980 --> 00:19:45,020
Easiest way to do this is to have a reference card.

295
00:19:45,020 --> 00:19:48,300
Another way is to have a memory tool to recall them all.

296
00:19:48,300 --> 00:19:52,140
One example of this is Murders Inc. or Murders Link.

297
00:19:52,140 --> 00:19:59,380
M for Muscular, U for Urinary, R for Respiratory, D for Digestive, E for Endocrine, second R

298
00:19:59,380 --> 00:20:05,700
for Reproductive, I for Integmentary, N for Neurologic, and C for Circulation, both cardiovascular

299
00:20:05,700 --> 00:20:06,700
and lymphatic.

300
00:20:06,700 --> 00:20:13,300
Or if we are doing the Murders Link variation, L is for Lymphatic and C is for Cardiovascular.

301
00:20:13,300 --> 00:20:18,620
Now, if you are making a reference card, it might help to order them in some sort of way

302
00:20:18,620 --> 00:20:20,300
that makes more sense than the Murders Inc.

303
00:20:20,300 --> 00:20:21,300
tool.

304
00:20:21,300 --> 00:20:26,180
As we demonstrated in the example, handoff reports using the system often begin with

305
00:20:26,180 --> 00:20:30,720
neuro, respiratory, cardiovascular, and then move on from there.

306
00:20:30,720 --> 00:20:35,860
This sort of matches the level of consciousness and ABC approach we use in EMS.

307
00:20:35,860 --> 00:20:38,260
Whatever way you choose to organize is fine.

308
00:20:38,260 --> 00:20:42,980
The idea is that the systems-based approach is useful for ongoing management or accepting

309
00:20:42,980 --> 00:20:46,180
a handover from another crew or staff.

310
00:20:46,180 --> 00:20:50,300
The algorithm-based approach we teach in EMS is well-suited for initial encounters

311
00:20:50,300 --> 00:20:53,560
or acutely decompensating patients.

312
00:20:53,560 --> 00:20:57,820
Another thing we can work into the systems-based approach is management of all the machines

313
00:20:57,820 --> 00:20:59,320
and devices.

314
00:20:59,320 --> 00:21:03,220
This is particularly useful when picking up a patient from an ICU.

315
00:21:03,220 --> 00:21:06,820
Some folks prefer to address this stuff after an assessment.

316
00:21:06,820 --> 00:21:09,340
Some opt to work them into the assessment.

317
00:21:09,340 --> 00:21:12,880
Others tend to divvy up the machine or device management and patient assessment between

318
00:21:12,880 --> 00:21:15,380
partners in a two-human crew.

319
00:21:15,380 --> 00:21:19,540
That's totally okay to do, but does lend itself to oversight.

320
00:21:19,540 --> 00:21:24,540
Just be sure that things like drug doses on pumps, vent settings, output in containers,

321
00:21:24,540 --> 00:21:29,420
etc., are all incorporated into our understanding of what is going on with the patient.

322
00:21:29,420 --> 00:21:35,940
And if it helps to address those things during review of systems, have at it.

323
00:21:35,940 --> 00:21:39,980
Just to summarize up to this point, we've got the algorithm-based assessment from our

324
00:21:39,980 --> 00:21:47,140
fine folks in EMS that works well for initial encounters or acute decompensation and a systems-based

325
00:21:47,140 --> 00:21:51,300
approach from the nursing friends for ongoing assessment or transfer of care from one provider

326
00:21:51,300 --> 00:21:52,880
to another.

327
00:21:52,880 --> 00:21:57,020
This delineation will not set in stone and definitely not to be taken as the one and

328
00:21:57,020 --> 00:22:01,740
only way to approach patient assessment in critical care transport may help make sense

329
00:22:01,740 --> 00:22:04,180
of things for some listeners out there.

330
00:22:04,180 --> 00:22:08,180
No one system is going to meet every need, but this does seem to work pretty well.

331
00:22:09,180 --> 00:22:14,020
Now that we have a general framework for working through this process in theory, the next part

332
00:22:14,020 --> 00:22:17,260
of this whole thing is to get it into clinical practice.

333
00:22:17,260 --> 00:22:22,220
One way to start is to simply memorize both the NREMT patient assessment algorithms and

334
00:22:22,220 --> 00:22:25,660
all the body systems and, again, putting them to work.

335
00:22:25,660 --> 00:22:29,300
Another option is to have a reference card on hand and get in the habit of using that

336
00:22:29,300 --> 00:22:31,260
to guide clinical practice.

337
00:22:31,260 --> 00:22:36,320
This works better for the systems-based approach when time likely isn't of the essence, so

338
00:22:36,320 --> 00:22:40,360
maybe we've memorized the EMS approach and reference card the other.

339
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:45,240
Or better yet, let's memorize them both and also have reference cards as a backup.

340
00:22:45,240 --> 00:22:49,900
We all know that memory is finicky under stress and we can plan ahead for that.

341
00:22:49,900 --> 00:22:55,040
Another option might be to build out and record a script as we discussed in a previous episode

342
00:22:55,040 --> 00:22:56,480
with Richard.

343
00:22:56,480 --> 00:23:00,680
This can help us visualize working through the steps in a guided practice so that we

344
00:23:00,680 --> 00:23:04,680
have a clear idea of how to work through these processes when on scene.

345
00:23:04,680 --> 00:23:09,640
We've spent quite a bit of time discussing this idea and giving examples in that episode,

346
00:23:09,640 --> 00:23:13,480
so if this appeals to you and you haven't yet given it a listen, definitely check that

347
00:23:13,480 --> 00:23:14,480
out.

348
00:23:14,480 --> 00:23:16,880
It was the second episode we put out.

349
00:23:16,880 --> 00:23:19,320
And if you do decide to build one, let us know.

350
00:23:19,320 --> 00:23:22,680
Would be cool to hear about it and discuss.

351
00:23:22,680 --> 00:23:26,800
There's also the option to practice this sort of stuff in a controlled setting.

352
00:23:26,800 --> 00:23:31,320
If you're a nursing person and new to the way we do assessment in EMS, get reps in on

353
00:23:31,320 --> 00:23:34,760
a mannequin or a partner or a puppy.

354
00:23:34,760 --> 00:23:38,220
Getting used to saying the steps out loud and physically working through the head to

355
00:23:38,220 --> 00:23:43,200
toe piece of the assessment will help dial it in and build muscle memory so that doesn't

356
00:23:43,200 --> 00:23:45,840
require conscious effort on scene.

357
00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:49,480
And make use of colleagues with EMS backgrounds at work if you can.

358
00:23:49,480 --> 00:23:52,840
There's likely tips and tricks to be learned from coworkers.

359
00:23:52,840 --> 00:23:57,400
And then for EMS people getting into critical care transport stuff and new to this idea

360
00:23:57,400 --> 00:24:02,280
of a systems based approach, consider putting time in to study up on each system included

361
00:24:02,280 --> 00:24:03,840
in the assessment.

362
00:24:03,840 --> 00:24:08,360
For example, when assessing or considering the urinary system, it helps to know what

363
00:24:08,360 --> 00:24:11,520
a normal urine output per unit time is.

364
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:14,880
And also how this varies by age and clinical condition.

365
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:19,420
If you've never emptied a Foley, watch a YouTube video or have someone show you so you don't

366
00:24:19,420 --> 00:24:22,920
spill pee on your foot the first time you try to figure it out.

367
00:24:22,920 --> 00:24:27,000
Maybe brush up on how to interpret a urinalysis so that you have a better idea of what might

368
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:29,160
be going on with the patient.

369
00:24:29,160 --> 00:24:33,200
Consider the different causes of hematuria and which might be relevant in transport so

370
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:36,400
that you can readily work through a differential if you notice it.

371
00:24:36,400 --> 00:24:40,860
The idea here is that we can continually expand our understanding of each system in an effort

372
00:24:40,860 --> 00:24:45,440
to glean more information from the systems review when time allows.

373
00:24:45,440 --> 00:24:50,000
All right, a few miscellaneous closing thoughts.

374
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:55,480
First is that while the NREMT patient assessment skill sheets have sort of defined how we teach

375
00:24:55,480 --> 00:25:00,400
and do patient assessment in EMS for some time, these things will be getting phased

376
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:02,160
out in the coming years.

377
00:25:02,160 --> 00:25:06,200
This isn't to say that how we do the thing will change in any way, just that the way

378
00:25:06,200 --> 00:25:11,560
EMS programs evaluate competency at the end of training programs is undergoing a redesign

379
00:25:11,560 --> 00:25:15,640
and the NREMT intends to do away with the psychomotor exam as we know it.

380
00:25:15,640 --> 00:25:19,360
If you're interested in learning more about that process, get in touch.

381
00:25:19,360 --> 00:25:24,440
We have an in on the process and can facilitate getting your take and opinions on the concept

382
00:25:24,440 --> 00:25:26,520
onto the drawing table.

383
00:25:26,520 --> 00:25:32,000
Next, recognize that the way we've outlined things here is just one opinion on the idea.

384
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,960
If you've got a system that works well, stick with it.

385
00:25:34,960 --> 00:25:38,880
If you've got thoughts on how we can improve our concept, let us know.

386
00:25:38,880 --> 00:25:44,000
Other frameworks for organizing the process, such as March or anything similar, can also

387
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:45,160
be used.

388
00:25:45,160 --> 00:25:49,200
Whatever the case may be, the last thing we want to be doing is entering into a scene

389
00:25:49,200 --> 00:25:54,640
or transport without a framework for working through all the steps involved in the process.

390
00:25:54,640 --> 00:25:59,480
Have a system, intentionally make an effort to dial it in, and stick with it consistently

391
00:25:59,480 --> 00:26:01,480
and as often as possible.

392
00:26:01,480 --> 00:26:03,280
If not, we will make mistakes.

393
00:26:03,280 --> 00:26:05,400
It's only a matter of time.

394
00:26:05,400 --> 00:26:09,720
Relying on one's improvisation skills in an unpredictable and potentially stressful environment

395
00:26:09,720 --> 00:26:12,000
is not the way to go.

396
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:15,240
And then actual very last thing, and then we'll stop talking.

397
00:26:15,240 --> 00:26:20,640
We mentioned early on the idea of a general impression from the inner EMT exam and how

398
00:26:20,640 --> 00:26:25,320
that's similar to the across-the-room assessment and the pediatric assessment triangle as it

399
00:26:25,320 --> 00:26:27,080
is taught in PALS.

400
00:26:27,080 --> 00:26:31,960
That triangle, for those who want one last tidbit of trivia, consists of appearance,

401
00:26:31,960 --> 00:26:34,200
work of breathing, and circulation.

402
00:26:34,200 --> 00:26:38,560
It's a way to systematically consider severity of the situation when looking at a child in

403
00:26:38,560 --> 00:26:40,240
the clinical setting.

404
00:26:40,240 --> 00:26:44,400
This same general strategy can be applied to the critical care transport setting.

405
00:26:44,400 --> 00:26:48,680
Oftentimes, it helps to stand at the door to a patient room or look through a window

406
00:26:48,680 --> 00:26:51,840
and take a moment to observe before diving in.

407
00:26:51,840 --> 00:26:57,120
So if we choose to organize assessment in this setting using a systems-based approach,

408
00:26:57,120 --> 00:27:01,280
consider keeping that general impression step or even the whole primary survey at the very

409
00:27:01,280 --> 00:27:04,080
beginning.

410
00:27:04,080 --> 00:27:05,720
And that's pretty much it.

411
00:27:05,720 --> 00:27:06,720
Thanks for tuning in.

412
00:27:06,720 --> 00:27:10,960
But real quick, before we go, just a few shoutouts.

413
00:27:10,960 --> 00:27:16,040
First is to Pragmatic Paramedics, another awesome podcast that we highly recommend.

414
00:27:16,040 --> 00:27:19,360
Next is to the St. Fisher Church of Evidence-Based Medicine.

415
00:27:19,360 --> 00:27:22,600
If you don't follow them already, definitely check them out.

416
00:27:22,600 --> 00:27:27,400
These guys, both Pragmatic Paramedics and the St. Fisher folks, have been instrumental

417
00:27:27,400 --> 00:27:31,240
in getting our stuff out there into the world and we truly appreciate that.

418
00:27:31,240 --> 00:27:35,160
And then we'll end things with a shoutout to my good friend Josh Pittman and his buddy

